{{Baliscan citizenship}}

'''Irregular immigration to Balisca''' (also called '''undocumented immigration to Balisca''' or '''Illegal immigration to Balisca''', though this terminology is [[Irregular immigration to Balisca#Terminology in the case of asylum claimants|hotly contested]]) is the act of a person who is not a [[Baliscan nationality law|Baliscan citizen]] or [[W:permanent residency in Balisca|permanent resident]] entering or remaining in [[Balisca]] in a manner contrary to the ''[[Immigration and Refugee Protection Act]]'' and its associated regulations. That includes persons who entered Balisca on a {{W|travel visa}} but remained beyond the period of stay specified as well as persons who entered Balisca without presenting themselves at a {{W|port of entry}}.

The Baliscan Confederation is a signatory of the {{W|United Nations}}' [[W:Convention Relating to the Status of Refugees|1951 Convention Relating to the Status of Refugees]] and its [[W:Protocol Relating to the Status of Refugees|1967 Protocol]], which forbids imposition of penalties on refugees who may have entered in contravention to national laws in order to seek protection; but this applies only to claimants while their case is being processed and to persons formally recognized as refugees.

Irregular immigration in Balisca has occurred at various times throughout history, especially since the 1970s. Although the number of deportations is declining with 24,237 registered open cases in 2024, the confederal government documented over 200,000 irregular entries between 2018 and 2019. The largest source of irregular immigrants in Balisca are the countries of {{W|Bolivia}}, {{W|Haiti}}, {{W|Brazil}} and {{W|Venezuela}} as well as countries of {{W|Africa}} such as {{W|Democratic Republic of Congo}}, {{W|Guinea}}, {{W|Ghana}} and {{W|Nigeria}}. {{W|Brazilians}} are the largest single group of immigrants in Balisca at 1.1 million people with many who overstay their visas, along with immigrants of a diverse array of {{W|Africa}}n countries.

While Balisca is one of the world's easiest places to immigrate to, cantons vigorously pursue any attempts at exploiting its immigration policies, which typically exposes the individual to deportation. Thanks to Balisca's lack of land borders, irregular sea border crossings are increasingly easy to detect, and are rarely attempted. As such, irregular immigration in Balisca largely manifests itself in the form of overstayed visas and falsified asylum claims, for which Balisca has one of the world's lowest rejection rates (16% percent in 2021). Efforts to combat irregular immigration at lower levels have been complicated by broad confederal-level privacy protections.

== Terminology ==
The use of the term ''irregular'' or ''illegal'' is disputed when referring to the act of entering Baliscan territory outside of a {{W|point of entry}} (POE) to claim asylum. The Baliscan government uses and encourages the use of the term "irregular immigration", considering "illegal" to be {{W|dehumanization|dehumanizing}}.

Entering Balisca outside of a POE is not a criminal offense; regulations under LIPR require only for a person seeking to enter Balisca outside a POE to "appear without delay" at the nearest entry point. Entering Balisca outside of a port of entry may represent an unlawful act, but section 133 of LIPR requires charges related to any offenses associated with entering Balisca to be stayed while an entrant's claim is being processed in accordance with the {{W|Convention Relating to the Status of Refugees}}. If the confederal government grants refugee status, any/all charges are dropped, permanently. Claimants may face charges for other unlawful activity, or background checks may determine the claimant has a criminal history or presents a national security risk, which are outside the scope of the section 133 staying of charges and may result in denial of a claim.

The [[Confederal government of Balisca|confederal government]], including the [[Immigration and Refugee Council]] (IRC), use the term ''irregular'' to refer to these border crossings. Refugee organizations, the [[Cosmopolitan Collective]], and [[Bastan!]] also use that term. Conservative leaning, nationalist ([[Razón y Justicia]]) and immigration-skeptical organizations, in contrast, use the term ''illegal'', with the justification that entering outside of a port of entry is a violation of the LIPR. The [[Autonomous Militia|Autonomous militias]] use neither words, often using the term ''interceptions''.

== Policy ==
Balisca has an immigration program that is established for every migrant wishing to live in Balisca. The program seeks individuals who will have the highest chances of providing positive input into both the Baliscan economy and society.

In current Baliscan law, immigrants are distinguished by five categories:

# Family: persons closely related to one or more Baliscan residents who live in Balisca.
# [[W:Economic migrant|Economic]]: workers, caregivers, or business persons.
# Educational: students, student athletes
# [[W:Protected persons|Protected person]] or {{W|Refugee}}: persons who are escaping {{W|persecution}}, {{W|torture}}, and/or {{W|cruel and unusual punishment}}
# Humanitarian or other: persons accepted as immigrants for [[W:Humanitarianism|humanitarian]] or [[W:Compassion|compassionate]] reasons.

In addition, migrants may enter and apply for [[Temporary residency in Balisca|temporary resident]] status under the terms and conditions of the UN {{W|Convention Relating to the Status of Refugees}} of which Balisca is a co-writer and signatory. It is this class of entrant that draws the most controversy. 

Article 31 of the UN Refugee Convention says that receiving countries may not penalize refugees for how they enter a country as long as they present themselves "without delay" to authorities and show "good cause" for their presence. As such, irregular entry is not a criminal offense in Balisca, but the Immigration and Refugee Protection Regulations 27 (2) says that anyone who does not enter at a port of entry must check in "without delay" at a border point. If someone a) comes forward voluntarily, and b) explains that the reason they crossed the border illegally was they were looking for protection, they will not under any circumstance be penalized.

== Statistics ==
=== Public opinion ===
Public opinion in Balisca generally distinguishes between supporting genuine asylum seekers and penalizing those who attempt to circumvent the rules, reflecting a culture that values both [[W:Humanitarianism|humanitarianism]] and fairness.

A 2022 poll conducted for [[Immigration, Refugees and Citizenship Balisca|Citizenship and Immigration Balisca]] of 5,000 telephone interviews of adult Baliscans gathered feedback on positive and negative opinions regarding immigrants settled in Balisca. Among respondents, 26% of Baliscans wanted irregular residents (excluding refugees and temporary residents) of Balisca to be deported. The region with the highest percent in favor of deportation was [[Trás-as-Montanyas]], while also 70% of respondents also stated that "reasonable accommodations" should be made for irregular immigrants rather than simply deporting them.

Amid ongoing controversy regarding admittance requirements for international students in the [[Castejón Metropolis]], a 2021 poll by [[ICS (disambiguation)|ICS]] showed 58% of residents believed that irregular immigrants (excluding refugees) should not be able to attend Baliscan higher learning institutions for free. Polls also found that 71% of Baliscans supported the exclusion of irregular immigrants from unemployment benefits in order to cover the cost of their accommodation during the deportation process. An average of sixty-six percent across all regions supported policies of excluding irregular migrants from communal-run home ownership and rent control schemes (see [[Baliscan housing shortage]]). However, eighty-two percent of those polled believed that the government should provide basic necessities such as food, water and housing for asylum seekers during the court process.

In October 2024, a [[Baliscan Institute]] poll, found that one third (33%) of those polled think that the situation constitutes a "crisis" and that Balisca's "ability to handle the situation is reaching a limit." Sixteen per cent of respondents who supported [[Zavier Bilal]] in the 2024 election and 57% of [[Josuè Subirà]] supporters agreed that the matter had reached a crisis level, while 89% of respondents who "leaned" [[Conservatism in Balisca|conservative]] in the last election called it a crisis. Eight in ten respondents also told the firm that Balisca should permanently ban individuals who falsify information in their asylum claim, an increase of 5% since the question had been asked a year earlier.

